  "reasoning": "The patient presents with persistent chest pain and nausea, which are hallmark symptoms of gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD). In GERD, stomach acid refluxes into the esophagus, causing significant chest discomfort (often described as burning or pressure) and triggering nausea. The accompanying shivering and anxiety are common secondary responses to chronic pain, discomfort, and the autonomic nervous system's reaction to digestive distress. Conditions like pneumonia or malaria typically feature prominent fever and cough, which are absent here. Peptic ulcer disease usually emphasizes epigastric/stomach pain rather than chest pain, and allergies would typically include respiratory signs like wheezing or throat swelling. Therefore, the clinical picture strongly points to gastroesophageal reflux disease.",
  "final_answer": "[DIAGNOSIS]gastroesophageal reflux disease[/DIAGNOSIS]"
}
